course: dictionary
lesson: 894
title: One word, several meanings
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Some words have more than one meaning, and the context tells you which one is meant. The word biscuit is a good example. Look at the words around it before you decide what it means.
explain: «Mum gave the dog a biscuit from the packet on the shelf» does not mean a sweet cake for a person. The dog and the word dog are the clues. Here, biscuit is a hard treat for dogs.
explain: Notice when a word has a capital letter. In the story about Megan's family, «Biscuit» is the name of their old dog, so it is not a food at all. A capital letter at the start of a word often means it is a name.
fact: Context means the words and sentences around a word.
fact: A biscuit is a small flat sweet baked treat in the UK, Ireland, Australia and New Zealand. In the USA it is called a cookie.
fact: In the USA and Canada, a biscuit is a small soft bread roll, often eaten with gravy.
fact: A dog biscuit is a hard treat for dogs.
fact: A good check is to swap your meaning into the sentence and see if it still makes sense.
exercise: Mum gives the dog a biscuit from the jar. What is the biscuit? | A soft bread roll with gravy | *A hard treat for dogs | A tin for keeping biscuits | A small flat sweet treat for people
exercise: In Canada, Sam eats a biscuit with his gravy at dinner. What is a biscuit here? | A tin for keeping sweets | A chocolate bar | A sweet flat cookie | *A small soft bread roll
exercise: Ella has a cup of tea and a chocolate biscuit. What does biscuit mean in this sentence? | A soft bread roll | A packet of tea | *A small flat sweet treat | A hard treat for dogs
test: What is a UK biscuit called in the USA? | Crumb | Cracker | Bun | *Cookie
test: What is a dog biscuit? | A bowl for food | A small sweet cake for children | A soft bread roll with gravy | *A hard treat for dogs
exam: Megan says Biscuit is asleep in his basket and can hardly stand up, so is biscuit a food here? | No, it is an empty biscuit tin | Yes, a biscuit to eat | *No, Biscuit is the dog's name, shown by the capital letter | Yes, a soft bread roll
